<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>12</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Every file of this uplink EXCEPT the one that reaches the game compiles
       here, which is the point of it. The guard needs only System.Reflection;
       the observation, the builder and the reflection walk need nothing but the
       framework; the uplink needs Sitrep.Contract for ISitrepUplink.

       PrincipiaUplink.Observer.cs, FlightPlannerHook.cs, PrincipiaSettingsSource.cs,
       PrincipiaGravityModelSource.cs and PrincipiaSession.Live.cs are deliberately
       ABSENT. They are the only files naming a KSP or Harmony type, and both
       PrincipiaUplink and PrincipiaSession are partial precisely so that omitting
       them still compiles: the AttachObserver and AttachGravityModel partial method
       calls disappear and the publish rule is driven by a fake observer instead, and
       the sourcing protocol runs against a fake plugin with no way to find a real
       one. Adding any of them here would trade that coverage for a reference this
       project does not need.

       The gravity model splits the same way and for the same reason. The GameDatabase
       walk is in PrincipiaGravityModelSource and is absent; every decision about what
       a config value means is in GravityModelParser and is here, driven against the
       shipped file's own spellings.

       Note how little the settings work left in the game-facing file: finding
       the addon, the game's body table, and the game's own target. Every read,
       every match and every publish decision is in the four files above it and is
       driven here by doubles that carry the producer's member names.

       The sourcing layer is here in full apart from that one file, which is what
       makes the precondition protocol testable at all: the whole of it (the
       version gate, the frame, the gates, the cursors and the refusal register)
       needs nothing but the framework.

       Selective Compile, the pattern the sibling uplink Tests projects use. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\PrincipiaVersionGuard.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\PrincipiaUplink.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\IFlightPlanObserver.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\FlightPlanObservation.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\FlightPlanBuilder.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\ReflectedMembers.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\FlightPlanReflection.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\ISettingsSource.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\SettingsObservation.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\SettingsReflection.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\NativeSettingsReader.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\SettingsBuilder.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\PrincipiaCalls.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\IPrincipiaPlugin.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\PrincipiaSession.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\PrincipiaFrame.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\ReflectedPrincipiaPlugin.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\GonogoPrincipiaUplink\GravityModelParser.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Sitrep.Contract\Sitrep.Contract.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
